import { WebReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> WebReaderCopy = WebReaderCopy {
  summary: "Build HTML values with ordinary functions, then serialize them separately from browser execution.",
  taskTitle: "Build and print a release-note card",
  purpose: "Start with one paragraph and print its HTML string. Turning an HTML description into a string is called server-side rendering (SSR) here. This example runs locally without a web server or browser.",
  setup: "import * as html gives std/web/html the local name html. The { children: ... } after html.p is a record with named fields; children supplies the paragraph text. Function arguments are separated by spaces.",
  after: [
    "fn paragraph defines a function with no arguments. -> html.Html<Unit> states its return type; Unit means there is no useful event payload here. The expression after = is the result. paragraph () calls the function; html.p constructs a description and renderToString returns a String. Neither operation prints or mounts anything.",
    "pub effect fn main is the program entry. println writes the string and a newline. The source text <notes> becomes &lt;notes&gt; in the output so that the text is not interpreted as an HTML tag.",
    "Now reuse one shape twice. fn card defines a function taking two String arguments, title and detail. -> html.Html<Unit> states its return type; the last expression in the block supplies that value. The heading and description annotations are needed by the tested CLI to determine the child types in this example.",
    "Two calls to card supply the children of fragment in source order. fragment adds no wrapper tag. page () calls the no-argument page function and returns the combined Html. A reusable component here is an ordinary function."
  ],
  details: [
    "If you put the nested h2 and p calls directly in the children array without annotations, the tested CLI 0.61.19 reports SES-T0201. Keep the Html<Unit> annotations on heading and description shown above. This form runs without adding events.",
    "For text beside an Html child in an array, first name the text with let label: html.Html<Unit> = html.text \"Release\", then use label as an array item. Keep that typed local: the tested CLI 0.61.19 rejects the directly nested text call in this mixed shape. A String used as the entire children value can be passed directly.",
    "The prop types catch mistakes such as a String for hidden. They do not guarantee every HTML nesting rule or accessibility requirement. For a button that changes the display, app below is the optional continuation."
  ],
  comparisonBefore: "TypeScript already represents card fields with an interface and reuses an ordinary function. This version returns the same two cards in the same order. escapeText is a small helper for &, < and > in text content.",
  comparisonAfter: "Both programs produce the same output. Seseragi supplies the Html description and serializer as standard APIs; this TS example writes the string construction and escaping helper itself. A TS template library can also supply escaping, and browser textContent safely sets text content."
}
